1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,372
There are now billions of cameras on Earth.

2
00:00:03,472 --> 00:00:06,490
They watch our streets, our shops, our workplaces.

3
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:10,737
But for most of history, not one of them understood a single thing it saw.

4
00:00:16,283 --> 00:00:24,781
In the summer of 1966, researchers at MIT set out to teach a computer to pick out objects in a picture.

5
00:00:24,881 --> 00:00:26,710
They gave themselves one summer.

6
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:28,601
It would take nearly half a century.

7
00:00:29,817 --> 00:00:32,596
Because a computer doesn't see a face, or a street.

8
00:00:32,696 --> 00:00:35,567
It sees a grid of numbers. Millions of them.

9
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:38,853
Each one, just a measure of red, green, or blue light.

10
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:43,581
For decades, engineers tried to write the rules by hand.

11
00:00:43,681 --> 00:00:44,926
Find the edges.

12
00:00:45,026 --> 00:00:46,313
Find the corners.

13
00:00:46,413 --> 00:00:49,166
Describe exactly what a person looks like.

14
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:53,550
But change the lighting, the angle, or the shadows, and the rules fall apart.

15
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:56,800
So researchers flipped the problem.

16
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:00,960
Instead of writing the rules, let the machine learn them, from examples.

17
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:05,747
Layers of artificial neurons, tuning themselves across millions of images.

18
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:13,423
In 2009, a project called ImageNet gathered more than fourteen million labeled photos.

19
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:19,718
In 2012, a neural network called AlexNet slashed the error rate in the ImageNet challenge.

20
00:01:19,818 --> 00:01:24,777
Three years later, machines were making fewer mistakes on that test than a trained human.

21
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:28,197
Today, that ability is everywhere.

22
00:01:28,297 --> 00:01:34,807
Faster than you can blink, a machine scans each frame and draws a box. Person. Car. Bicycle.

23
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:39,509
It counts shoppers in a store, and warns when a worker steps into a danger zone.

24
00:01:40,717 --> 00:01:43,336
But a machine that can see can also watch.

25
00:01:43,436 --> 00:01:49,061
The same technology that finds a face in a crowd can be used to recognize whose face it is.

26
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:50,848
Machines have learned to see.

27
00:01:50,948 --> 00:01:53,327
The question now is what we let them look at.

